1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,743
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,743 --> 00:00:07,555
This is chapter eight: collectors and stream composition.

3
00:00:08,005 --> 00:00:16,296
Most developers learn three collectors, toList, toSet and toMap, and then drop out of the stream to finish the job with a loop.

4
00:00:16,296 --> 00:00:25,370
That's exactly where streams stop paying off, because grouping, partitioning and multi-level aggregation are what collect was designed for.

5
00:00:26,270 --> 00:00:29,925
The task: total revenue per vendor, per delivery slot.

6
00:00:29,925 --> 00:00:40,009
The loop works, but it's nested container management, and every variation, a third level, a filter, a count instead of a sum, means restructuring it.

7
00:00:40,459 --> 00:00:41,685
Reduce can't help.

8
00:00:41,685 --> 00:00:49,178
It expects a brand-new value at every step, so building a map with it means copying the map for every element.

9
00:00:49,178 --> 00:00:50,336
That's quadratic.

10
00:00:50,786 --> 00:00:52,514
Collect exists for this.

11
00:00:52,514 --> 00:00:58,272
It's mutable reduction: create one container, then mutate it as elements arrive.

12
00:00:58,272 --> 00:01:03,671
Use reduce for values, like a sum or a maximum, and collect for containers.

13
00:01:04,571 --> 00:01:06,671
A Collector isn't one function.

14
00:01:06,671 --> 00:01:07,348
It's five.

15
00:01:07,348 --> 00:01:11,547
The supplier creates an empty container, like a new ArrayList.

16
00:01:11,997 --> 00:01:16,307
The accumulator folds one element into it, like list dot add.

17
00:01:16,757 --> 00:01:19,090
The combiner merges two containers.

18
00:01:19,090 --> 00:01:23,690
That's only used in parallel, when each thread has its own container.

19
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:27,687
The finisher turns the container into the final result.

20
00:01:27,687 --> 00:01:29,815
For toList there's nothing to do.

21
00:01:30,265 --> 00:01:36,878
And characteristics are hints to the stream, like identity finish, meaning the container is the result.

22
00:01:37,328 --> 00:01:41,532
Remember this one fact: the combiner only runs in parallel.

23
00:01:41,532 --> 00:01:45,451
A sequential stream uses a single container throughout.

24
00:01:45,451 --> 00:01:51,792
So a broken combiner passes every test, then corrupts data the day someone adds parallel.

25
00:01:52,692 --> 00:01:55,837
Here's the feature that makes the API compose.

26
00:01:55,837 --> 00:02:04,383
groupingBy takes a classifier, and an optional second collector, called the downstream, that decides what each group becomes.

27
00:02:04,833 --> 00:02:08,383
With toList, each vendor maps to a list of lines.

28
00:02:08,383 --> 00:02:10,267
With counting, to a count.

29
00:02:10,267 --> 00:02:12,513
With mapping, to a set of SKUs.

30
00:02:12,963 --> 00:02:15,799
And the downstream can be another groupingBy.

31
00:02:15,799 --> 00:02:19,896
That's how you nest to any depth, with no special support needed.

32
00:02:20,796 --> 00:02:26,507
Revenue per vendor: group by vendor name, with a downstream reducing collector.

33
00:02:26,507 --> 00:02:33,085
The three-argument reducing is the one to remember: an identity, a mapper, and an operator.

34
00:02:33,085 --> 00:02:34,748
Zero, line total, plus.

35
00:02:35,198 --> 00:02:40,271
The two-level report from the loop is just a groupingBy inside a groupingBy.

36
00:02:40,271 --> 00:02:47,081
Past two levels, extract a record and a named method, because the type signature stops being readable.

37
00:02:47,531 --> 00:02:51,753
groupingBy returns a HashMap, so key order is unspecified.

38
00:02:51,753 --> 00:02:57,941
If order matters, pass a map factory like TreeMap new, instead of sorting afterwards.

39
00:02:58,841 --> 00:03:04,937
Three downstream collectors shape elements inside each group, after classification.

40
00:03:04,937 --> 00:03:10,152
Mapping transforms them, like collecting each vendor's SKUs into a set.

41
00:03:10,152 --> 00:03:14,265
Java 9 added flatMapping, which flattens, and filtering.

42
00:03:14,715 --> 00:03:16,583
Filtering is the subtle one.

43
00:03:16,583 --> 00:03:23,654
If you filter before grouping, vendors whose lines are all non-perishable disappear from the map entirely.

44
00:03:24,104 --> 00:03:30,439
If you use filtering as the downstream, every vendor stays as a key, some with an empty list.

45
00:03:30,439 --> 00:03:36,637
Which one you want depends on whether the report should show vendors with zero perishables.

46
00:03:36,637 --> 00:03:41,405
The two spellings look almost identical, which is how the bug happens.

47
00:03:42,305 --> 00:03:45,243
For a yes or no split, use partitioningBy.

48
00:03:45,243 --> 00:03:50,208
Split the cart into lines we can fulfil and lines that are backordered.

49
00:03:50,208 --> 00:03:54,615
It takes a downstream too, so you can count instead of listing.

50
00:03:55,065 --> 00:04:05,469
The reason to prefer it over groupingBy with a boolean: partitioningBy always returns both keys, true and false, even when one side is empty.

51
00:04:05,469 --> 00:04:10,339
groupingBy leaves absent groups out, so get false can return null.

52
00:04:11,239 --> 00:04:13,992
toMap has three separate failure modes.

53
00:04:13,992 --> 00:04:23,449
First, duplicate keys throw an IllegalStateException at runtime, on whatever data triggers it, which is rarely the data in your tests.

54
00:04:23,449 --> 00:04:28,178
Always supply a merge function unless keys are structurally unique.

55
00:04:28,628 --> 00:04:38,568
Second, null values throw a NullPointerException, even though a HashMap would accept them, because toMap uses map dot merge internally.

56
00:04:38,568 --> 00:04:40,703
Filter or default them first.

57
00:04:41,153 --> 00:04:46,141
Third, the result is a HashMap, so iteration order is unspecified.

58
00:04:46,141 --> 00:04:50,902
Pass LinkedHashMap new as the fourth argument if order matters.

59
00:04:51,802 --> 00:04:58,397
Java 12's teeing feeds every element to two collectors at once, then merges their results.

60
00:04:58,397 --> 00:05:03,599
Here, min and max in a single pass, combined into a price range record.

61
00:05:03,599 --> 00:05:08,655
Before Java 12 that meant two traversals or a hand-written collector.

62
00:05:09,105 --> 00:05:19,281
It's also the clean way to compute a ratio: count the perishable lines and count all lines in the same pass, then divide, guarding against zero.

63
00:05:19,731 --> 00:05:23,978
And collectingAndThen collects, then transforms the result.

64
00:05:23,978 --> 00:05:27,074
Here, each group becomes an immutable list.

65
00:05:27,074 --> 00:05:32,041
It allocates twice, which matters on a hot path but not for a report.

66
00:05:32,941 --> 00:05:37,965
Write your own collector only when no combination of the built-ins fits.

67
00:05:37,965 --> 00:05:40,337
A bounded top-N is a good example.

68
00:05:40,337 --> 00:05:42,640
The supplier is a priority queue.

69
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:48,292
The accumulator adds each item and evicts the smallest once it holds more than n.

70
00:05:48,292 --> 00:05:50,455
The finisher sorts what's left.

71
00:05:50,905 --> 00:05:55,254
It's worth it because sorted then limit buffers the entire stream.

72
00:05:55,254 --> 00:05:57,625
This keeps memory proportional to n.

73
00:05:58,075 --> 00:06:01,025
The combiner is the part people get wrong.

74
00:06:01,025 --> 00:06:06,996
It must be associative, and consistent with accumulating everything in one container.

75
00:06:06,996 --> 00:06:15,846
A combiner that just returns a silently drops everything in b, and only in parallel, so no sequential test will ever catch it.

76
00:06:16,746 --> 00:06:18,645
Two internals worth knowing.

77
00:06:18,645 --> 00:06:22,173
Characteristics: identity finish skips the finisher.

78
00:06:22,173 --> 00:06:24,954
Unordered makes parallel merging cheaper.

79
00:06:24,954 --> 00:06:31,060
And concurrent lets every thread write into one shared container, skipping the merge tree.

80
00:06:31,060 --> 00:06:35,469
That helps only on large parallel streams with many small groups.

81
00:06:35,469 --> 00:06:39,743
On a sequential stream, groupingByConcurrent is strictly worse.

82
00:06:40,193 --> 00:06:42,663
And the three ways to get a list.

83
00:06:42,663 --> 00:06:48,501
Collectors dot toList is mutable in practice, but the spec doesn't promise it.

84
00:06:48,501 --> 00:06:50,971
toUnmodifiableList rejects nulls.

85
00:06:50,971 --> 00:06:58,455
Stream dot toList, from Java 16, is unmodifiable and allows nulls, which makes it the right default.

86
00:06:59,355 --> 00:07:00,567
Production rules.

87
00:07:00,567 --> 00:07:03,064
Always give toMap a merge function.

88
00:07:03,064 --> 00:07:05,276
Use counting, not summing ones.

89
00:07:05,276 --> 00:07:10,555
Use summarizingInt or teeing when you need several aggregates in one pass.

90
00:07:10,555 --> 00:07:13,266
Pass a map factory when order matters.

91
00:07:13,266 --> 00:07:15,977
Name multi-level groupings as methods.

92
00:07:15,977 --> 00:07:19,259
And prefer Stream dot toList for simple lists.

93
00:07:19,709 --> 00:07:22,010
Never build a container with reduce.

94
00:07:22,010 --> 00:07:25,271
Never write a combiner you haven't thought through.

95
00:07:25,271 --> 00:07:29,491
Never use collect for side effects, like saving as you accumulate.

96
00:07:29,491 --> 00:07:31,792
Never assume groupingBy's key order.

97
00:07:31,792 --> 00:07:33,838
Never feed null values to toMap.

98
00:07:33,838 --> 00:07:40,487
And never hand-write a collector when mapping, filtering, teeing and collectingAndThen already cover it.

99
00:07:41,387 --> 00:07:48,470
Java 8 shipped the core collectors: groupingBy, partitioningBy, toMap, joining, mapping and reducing.

100
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:53,720
Java 9 added flatMapping and filtering for shaping inside groups.

101
00:07:54,170 --> 00:07:57,491
Java 10 added the unmodifiable collectors.

102
00:07:57,491 --> 00:08:00,101
Immutable, but they reject nulls.

103
00:08:00,551 --> 00:08:02,407
Java 12 added teeing.

104
00:08:02,857 --> 00:08:05,216
Java 16 added Stream dot toList.

105
00:08:05,216 --> 00:08:14,654
When migrating, check nobody adds to the result, because the old collector's list was mutable and the new one throws at runtime.

106
00:08:15,104 --> 00:08:23,153
And Java 24 finalised stream gatherers, the mirror image of custom collectors, for intermediate operations.

107
00:08:23,153 --> 00:08:25,259
That's chapter thirty-eight.

108
00:08:26,159 --> 00:08:26,780
To recap.

109
00:08:26,780 --> 00:08:30,786
Collect mutates a container, while reduce produces values.

110
00:08:30,786 --> 00:08:35,344
A collector is five parts, and the combiner only runs in parallel.

111
00:08:35,344 --> 00:08:39,142
Downstream collectors let groupingBy nest to any depth.

112
00:08:39,142 --> 00:08:44,460
Filtering as a downstream keeps empty groups, and filtering first drops them.

113
00:08:44,460 --> 00:08:50,607
And toMap needs a merge function, rejects nulls, and has no order unless you ask for one.

114
00:08:51,057 --> 00:08:54,374
Next is chapter nine, Optional and null strategy.

115
00:08:54,374 --> 00:08:55,729
Thanks for watching.
